WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.497
Warning! Warning!

2
00:00:01.497 --> 00:00:06.705
I built this with AI and text to speech, because I can't narrate for shit!

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.383
Hey, Dreamer here.

4
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:11.022
Last one of the season.

5
00:00:11.022 --> 00:00:16.581
We've built the voice, the jobs, the map, the look, the people and the player.

6
00:00:16.581 --> 00:00:21.498
Now we put it all together: where the story starts, and how it plays.

7
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.037
Open Game Start, then Story Starts.

8
00:00:25.037 --> 00:00:29.860
Voyage gives you one called Random, and here's ours, The Lion's Gone Walkabout,

9
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.523
set as the default, the one used when the host doesn't pick.

10
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:38.167
The description is the only part players read, when they pick a start.

11
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:40.027
Keep it to a sentence or three.

12
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:44.353
The story start is what the narrator turns into the first scene.

13
00:00:44.353 --> 00:00:49.696
Mine has the setup in square brackets, how you got here, and what's happening right now.

14
00:00:49.696 --> 00:00:51.639
At the end, a few opening hooks,

15
00:00:51.639 --> 00:00:56.314
so an escape artist and a knife thrower don't get the exact same first scene.

16
00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:59.659
Starting quests are handed out from turn one.

17
00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:05.509
That's how the lion job finally reaches the player, like we promised back in episode three.

18
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:09.946
And where it opens: Mudbury, at the show's camp.

19
00:01:09.946 --> 00:01:13.594
List several and Voyage picks one at random.

20
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.126
One thing to watch.

21
00:01:16.126 --> 00:01:20.750
Anyone standing in the start's area is in the opening scene every single time.

22
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.425
I live at the camp, so I'm always there, and that's the point.

23
00:01:24.425 --> 00:01:28.456
But don't park your secret villain in the starting area by accident.

24
00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:33.001
Game Modes. A mode is a way to play the same world.

25
00:01:33.001 --> 00:01:39.085
Ours are Opening Night, the show as it's meant to be, and Off Night, a quiet one around the wagons.

26
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:41.959
Difficulty sets how tough the fights are.

27
00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:47.579
Very easy halves the health of everyone you fight, and very hard makes it one and a half times.

28
00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:52.050
Instructions are extra notes for the narrator for the whole game,

29
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:57.612
on top of everything else you've written, so don't make them fight your world's own rules.

30
00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:04.197
And the ask the narrator prompt is the first thing players see when they open the help chat.

31
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.113
And that's season one.

32
00:02:07.113 --> 00:02:12.200
You've got a whole playable world, and it's all linked in the description.

33
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:17.013
Season two, we make it play: items, stats, moves, traits and triggers.

34
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:21.415
I'm Dreamer. Now go build something weird, and I'll see you next time!

